  "evidence": "TEXT_C: Bill of Rights guaranteeing housing, education, healthcare, decent work and a minimum wage; NHS rebuilt as an integrated public service with public health measures; raises to Child Benefit and pensions, restoration of benefits for 16–17s, universal Cold Climate Allowance, ending means tests for residential care; fair personal taxation including a 15p starter rate, wider basic band, removing the NI ceiling for high earners, and cutting VAT on domestic fuel; full-employment strategy with apprenticeships, skills and R&D; major investment in social housing and protections for new social homes. TEXT_G: Calls for a fair referendum on EU relationship focusing on sovereignty (federal Europe vs association of sovereign nations), urgency to decide before powers become irreversible, and criticism of main parties’ limited referendum on the single currency.",
  "reasoning": "The electorate’s top priorities are Care and Equality, and TEXT_C is saturated with concrete social-welfare, redistribution, and universal-service commitments that directly address reducing harm and promoting equal treatment/outcomes, with some Proportionality elements via skills, employment, and progressive tax changes. TEXT_G focuses mainly on Loyalty/sovereignty and procedural Authority concerns with little emphasis on Care or Equality, so it aligns less with the given moral profile.",
  "confidence": "high",
  "winner": "TEXT_C"
}
